The answer is that you can use the `lru_cache` decorator from the `functools` module, which caches the results of function calls so that they are not recomputed on subsequent calls. The `lru_cache` decorator uses a least-recently-used (LRU) cache to store the cached values, and it automatically evicts the least recently used value when the cache is full.

You can use the `@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=None)` decorator above your function definition to enable caching. The `maxsize` parameter specifies the maximum number of items that can be stored in the cache. If you set `maxsize` to `None`, the cache will grow indefinitely, but it may not be suitable for all use cases.

For example:
```
@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def factorial(n):
    if n < 2:
        return 1
    else:
        return n * factorial(n-1)
```
This will cache the results of `factorial` calls, so that subsequent calls with the same arguments will return the cached result instead of recomputing it.

